[Music]  over the past decade student loan debt  has skyrocketed in the u.s  by december 2020 43 million americans  owed the government a collective 1.6  trillion dollars  that's putting pressure on  president-elect joe biden  we know that in the 21st century 12  years of schooling is not enough  and that young people are getting  crushed by the burden of student debt  democrats want biden to issue an  executive order to forgive some loans if  a bill can't get through congress but  opponents say  that would unfairly assist high income  earners while increasing the existing  burden on taxpayers  what's happening is a lot of people are  not sending in payments to the education  department  about a third of the 1.6 trillion  dollars that's on the books currently is  not going to get repaid which basically  means taxpayers are  going to have to cover that somehow  so what will joe biden do first he wants  to bring back  a policy sought by congressional  democrats early in the pandemic  the legislation passed by the democratic  house calls for  immediate 10 000 forgiveness of student  loans  it should be done immediately from there  biden says he wants to start  by revamping the existing forgiveness  options offered by the federal  government  there are these programs in place called  income based repayment and the first of  these was actually  passed under the clinton administration  you know bill clinton actually liked the  idea of having  students actually pay a share of their  income each month rather than a fixed  dollar amount  one example of these programs is called  repay which stands for revised pay as  you earn  in repay plans anyone who's struggling  to pay their student loan  can set a new minimum payment that is 10  of their income over 25 000  if the borrower makes these payments on  time for 20 to 25 years  the government will forgive the  remaining balance of the loan now keep  in mind this is 10  of your discretionary income so for some  people it might not make sense if you're  making a lot of money  you know and um 10 actually might be  higher than what your standard repayment  is  so you kind of have to look at it and  see if it makes sense but anyone  can can do this um you're entitled to  enroll in this program and a lot of  people have enrollment in this program  has surged  but there's a catch the forgiven balance  of the loan is counted as income  on your taxes you know if you think  about it that tax bill can actually be  really high if you  if if it's let's say you owe a hundred  thousand dollars at the end of that  20-year program  well that's a hundred thousand dollars  of income and so that could be thousands  of dollars in  a tax bill that you face right then and  there at the end at the end of  your repayment plan biden's proposal  changes this program in a way that draws  less money from borrowers  the minimum payment rate for these plans  would be reduced from 10  to 5 percent of a borrower's income  after their first 25 thousand 000  earned and biden wants to make it so  that forgiven debt  won't be taxed as income biden also  wants to sweeten the terms of  forgiveness  for public sector workers like teachers  military personnel  and also employees of non-profits people  in these career fields already have  their debts forgiven tax-free  after 10 years of public service but  biden wants to relieve  some of their debts on a shorter  timeline biden has said  that the current program isn't working  as intended there is a program that  exists now under the law that forgives  student loans  for being able to engage in engaging in  public service  i'm going to institute that fundamental  change in that so it's able to be  available to everyone  that in fact is engaged it's not being  very well managed right now so i'm going  to do all of those things  some say that contractors are to blame  for the management issues  the contractors that the education  department  uses to deal with students who have  student loans  have gotten a lot of things wrong so  what you've seen is there have been a  lot of  instances of where people thought that  they were on track to get forgiveness  and then they learned that they're not  and they maybe have to start all over  so this program has turned out to be a  mess and he wants to fix that  members of both political parties think  that the federal student loan program  needs an update one of the big  differences right now is on the  democratic side they're saying  cancel student debt right now and then  we'll worry about reforming the program  republicans are saying  wait a minute the first thing we need to  do is fix this program  to solve the underlying problems in the  first place any student loan debt that's  forgiven  is covered by taxpayers mr biden says  he'll pay for expanded debt forgiveness  by reversing parts of the tax cuts of  2017.  for a lot of his plans he wants to you  know raise taxes on high earners on our  corporations corporate profits  so more broadly he's talked about doing  that  another issue is who exactly benefits  from expanded loan forgiveness programs  most student debt is actually held by  people who are in upper income  households  the brookings institution shows that uh  60 percent of student debt so more than  half of the 1.6 trillion on the books  is held by people who are making 74 000  a year or more but if debts are forgiven  the smaller payouts given to lower  income households are likely to have a  bigger impact on that household's  finances  in recent years roughly one in five  student loan borrowers  fell behind on their payments according  to the federal reserve  bank of new york that's a sign that many  remain at risk of default on their  student loan which could impact their  credit  or even lead to garnished wages if you  look at the number of people that have  defaulted on their loans  that's almost the same as the number of  people who lost their homes during the  housing crash  this was almost unheard of you know in  the early 2000s that there would be  someone calling for  canceling student debt so it shows just  how much of a  middle class anxiety this has become in  just the past 10 years  and biden hopes to relieve that anxiety  over the debts in his first term

